            <ArticleTitle>Gastric lipoma: an unusual cause of dyspeptic symptoms.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2016-215297</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr2016215297</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Gastric lipomas are rare tumours accounting for 2-3% of all benign gastric tumours. These are usually submucosal in origin but in rare cases may be subserosal in origin. Although a majority of gastric lipomas are asymptomatic and usually detected incidentally, occasionally these can cause abdominal pain, dyspeptic disorders, obstruction, invagination and haemorrhages. In the literature, only 200 cases of gastric lipomas have been reported so far. We report a case of a 56-year-old female with a submucosal symptomatic gastric lipoma presenting with disabling dyspeptic symptoms.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>2016 BMJ Publishing Group Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
